# Sofie Open Live Gateway (`eyevinn-sofie-tsr-open-live`)

sofie-tsr-open-live is a TSR plugin for Sofie Automation that lets Sofie drive an Open Live production over Open Live’s controller WebSocket using the 1.x automation control contract. In practice, it translates Sofie timeline objects into concrete Open Live commands for program and preview switching, DSK and graphics toggling, audio level/mute changes (with optional ramps), clip cue/play/pause/stop, on-air control, and fade-to-black. It also handles reconnects by waiting for the HELLO/SNAPSHOT sequence, resetting to the state Open Live reports, and then only sending the differences—plus keep-alives and command ACK/NACK tracking so Sofie can surface command errors.

As a managed service on Eyevinn Open Source Cloud (OSC), you can provision it without building and operating your own playout gateway image and runtime. The gateway connects out to Sofie Core and to Open Live (no inbound ports required), and OSC mode authentication is supported via exchanging your PAT for a SAT and using the osc.bearer subprotocol—so you can run it in the same environment as the rest of your OSC services while keeping configuration in Sofie where it belongs (Open Live URL, production ID, and token in device settings).

[Source](https://github.com/Eyevinn/sofie-tsr-open-live)

## Example

```terraform
resource "osc_instance" "example" {
  service_id = "eyevinn-sofie-tsr-open-live"
  name       = "mylive"

  parameters = {
    CoreHost = "..."
    # CorePort = "..."
    # DeviceId = "..."
    # LogLevel = "..."
  }

  sensitive_parameters = {
    DeviceToken = var.device_token
    OpenLiveOscPat = var.open_live_osc_pat
    OpenLiveApiKey = var.open_live_api_key
  }
}
```

Commented lines are optional parameters. Values marked `"..."` need a real value. Outputs are `url`, `external_ip`, `external_port` and the raw `instance` document, see the `osc_instance` resource.

## Parameters

| Name | Type | Required | Description |
|---|---|---|---|
| `CoreHost` | string | yes | Hostname of the Sofie Core the gateway connects to. On OSC use Core's cluster-internal host from the Core instance details; the public URL is behind the OSC auth gate and does not work. Feeds CORE_HOST. |
| `CorePort` | string | no | Port of Sofie Core, for example 3000. Defaults to 3000 if empty. Feeds CORE_PORT. |
| `DeviceId` | string | no | Stable ID this gateway registers with in Sofie Core. If empty, the first label of the instance hostname is used. Feeds DEVICE_ID. |
| `DeviceToken` | string | yes | Secret the gateway uses to register with Sofie Core. Any stable value works; keep it the same across restarts. Store it as a service secret. Feeds DEVICE_TOKEN. Sensitive, set it in `sensitive_parameters`. |
| `LogLevel` | string | no | Log level of the gateway. Defaults to info in this image. Feeds LOG_LEVEL. |
| `OpenLiveOscPat` | string | no | OSC Personal Access Token the gateway uses to log in to Open Live when the Open Live device in Sofie is set to osc auth mode. Store it as a service secret. Keeps the token out of Sofie's device settings, where the gateway would log it. Feeds OPEN_LIVE_OSC_PAT. Sensitive, set it in `sensitive_parameters`. |
| `OpenLiveApiKey` | string | no | API key the gateway sends to a self-hosted Open Live when the Open Live device in Sofie is set to apiKey auth mode. Store it as a service secret. Keeps the key out of Sofie's device settings, where the gateway would log it. Feeds OPEN_LIVE_API_KEY. Sensitive, set it in `sensitive_parameters`. |

The instance `name` must match `^\w+$` and be unique per service within the workspace. OSC lowercases it and allows at most 20 characters.

Parameter values are strings; booleans take `"true"` or `"false"`. Reference an `osc_secret` as `osc_secret.<name>.ref` to keep a value out of the instance configuration. This page is generated from the OSC catalog; the provider validates against the live catalog when planning.
